# The four workload shapes that are a BLOCK — `step` and `spike`, each in both units.
#
# These are the ones `M224` `A` had to fix the splice for. A `step`/`spike` block's span runs into
# the next line's indentation, so an edit that read its end off the span swallowed the line below
# it; `replaceWorkload` reads the end off the node's own offsets instead. Change a stage's target
# here, or press `+ stage` / `✕`, and the block is rewritten whole — the rest of the test does not
# move.
#
# The spelling of a stage belongs to its block, not to the stage: a `step` stage is always
# `to N for <dur>`, and a `spike` stage is `hold N for <dur>` when it jumps and `to N over <dur>`
# when it ramps. That is why the band's stage row offers `jump`/`ramp` on a spike and not on a
# step — the grid is the grammar, not a decoration on it.

@catalogue @workload
test "readers arrive in two steps"
  step users
    to 2 for 1s
    to 4 for 1s
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@catalogue @workload
test "the arrival rate climbs a staircase"
  step rps
    to 10 for 1s
    to 20 for 1s
  api GET /categories
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

# The shape a real traffic spike has: sit flat, jump, ramp back down. A `spike` block is the only
# one whose stages mix the two modes, which is the case the band's stage editor exists for.
@health @workload
test "a burst of readers, then a climb away from it"
  spike users
    hold 2 for 1s
    to 6 over 1s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration is less than 2s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@health @workload
test "a burst of arrivals a second, then a climb away from it"
  spike rps
    hold 8 for 1s
    to 20 over 1s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%
